blic would  approve that was the metric by which a  government was judged and seemingly  correctly and indeed rewarded  politically and there were parallels in  the United States with with Clinton's  argument with the bushes Germany you  know Gerhard schröder then Merkel it was  all about competence and something has  now drastically changed and the question  is has it changed because people have  discovered other values I think probably  not although it appears so religion  culture these issues have become more  dominant or is it that people have  reacted to economic failure by looking  at at other at other things and I think  it is the latter and the crucial event  was 2008 the collapse of the banking  system a fall in our economy of I think  5% something it never happened before  since the second world war had a massive  impact on people's living standards on  the ability of government to deliver  services it happened here it happened  across the continent in the United  States and in many other places and I  think partly out of anger and  frustration after a time lag you've had  other kinds of popular politics coming  up and in the United States it's been a  combination of religious fundamentalism  and the kind of nativist politics that  trump is so successfully exploited here  it's brexit in germany it's sort of  anti-muslim anti-immigrant same in  france so we're getting these new  strains of politics coming in but i  think it is a reaction to perceived  economic failure and i think we will see  the proof of this in the next few years  because if Trump fails to deliver  people's living standards don't improve  in the United States if the wages of  American workers continue to stagnate or  fall I suspect they will turn against  him  and the test of populism will be whether  it does meet people's expectations about  living standards now that you could say  that's a sort of crudely economic view  and clearly other values do matter and  I'd like them to matter but I don't  think we should get carried away I'll  just take two examples I mean I would  like to feel that our politics is  becoming a bit greener we're becoming a  bit more environmentally aware climate  emergencies all those things but we've  had a couple of salutary warnings in the  last few months about what happens if  politicians get too carried away Macross  carbon tax resulted in the yellow vests  protest in part the Australian Labor  Party was widely tipped to win the  general election failed to do so at  least in part as a reaction against what  run doubtedly progressive green politics  and so although we've got to I mean I  somebody was on the center-left I would  describe myself as we've got to be alert  to issues around inequality environment  and so on I think we shouldn't get too  far ahead of public opinion where the  new politics is partly a reflection of  the anger that people have felt because  of economic failure Vince thank you very  much for setting out those thoughts at  Curtin straight to you  Richard Burgin obviously sitting in the  in the Shadow Cabinet do you think it's  fair to say that a new more radical  politics on left which many would argue  with Corbin Shadow Cabinet encompasses  is one that has downplayed economics in  terms of much more social radicalism  well I think that in the Labour Party  since Jeremy Corbyn became elected he's  made a central plank  along with the Shadow Chancellor John  MacDonald central plank of his politics  a clear anti austerity politics actually  Jeremy became leader of the Labour Party  on the basis that labour members and  supporters and affiliated trade unions  wanted a very hard very clear opposition  to austerity so in opposition to  austerity a politics for yes economic  growth but  more equally was the very center of his  prospectus and so therefore it's not the  case in my view that Jeremy or Labour  have downplayed the economic aspect but  in terms of the title of this debate and  discussion today a duty is a bit of a  false choice to pose social change  against economic growth whatever label  members of the public put on it I think  most people actually want both  they bought social change in other words  a better society and they also want  economic growth or to be more particular  rather economic change sort of economic  injustice in our society of course  effects people's levels of stress it  affects people's enjoyment of their work  and their day-to-day lives it affects  people's relationship it affects  people's health as well as so many  studies show but I think what people  aren't pursuing is really growth for  growth's sake and a book that finally to  get around to reading in the recess  amongst many others is the book about  donut economics she calls it by kate ray  worth and i think she puts it very  interestingly in there kate says that  humanity's 21st century challenge is to  meet the needs of all within the means  of the planet and when look at the  diagram that she drew up it really  doesn't make sense doesn't it if people  don't have enough and that includes by  the way a political voice housing health  and education and the rest of it they  fall inside the hole but if the economy  grows for the sake of growth in of  itself and isn't checked or regulated or  looked at and you spill over into  damaging that which we depend upon all  of us ant you manatee and the other  species and the globe which is our  environment so people want richer lives  that's intrinsically linked to the  economy and I don't think we can use the  term economy in a neutral sense what it  when we talk about economic growth we've  got to ask ourselves the question of  ownership in distribution what do we  mean by the economy and when growth  takes place who enjoys the fruits of  that growth because in recent decades do  to untrammeled neoliberal economics more  and more the benefits of that growth  have gone to the very richest people  both in our country but also globally as  well so people do want richer large so I  we need to start looking at trying to  reduce the amount of time that people's  day-to-day work or the working week  takes up the proliferation of zeros  contract it's right across right right  across the economic spectrum actually  even those who are relatively well-off  have their own stresses when it comes to  living standards when it comes to Social  Welfare no answer and on this really the  green new deal as proposed by AOC in the  United States and also by Rebecca on  Bell in the Labor Party here is an  important part of this debate but I  suppose I'll finish really with this  quote which i think is very helpful to  understanding this debate and the way I  think is for nuts by the respected  economists from Latin American forum  Ecuadorian President Rafael Correa who  said the great challenge facing Humanity  in the 21st century is to achieve the  supremacy of human beings over capital  with societies dominating markets not  markets dominating societies  you

